message.content:
You are adjudicating a reported finding about a C++ -> Rust port. Default to REFUTED
unless you can reproduce the problem yourself.


Ground rules:
- The C++ library header (include/indicators.hpp) does NOT exist in this repo. The only reference
  for library behaviour is the compiled binary /workspace/dataset/test2_executable (objdump/nm/gdb are available) and
  the caller /workspace/dataset/test2.cpp.
- Do NOT modify anything under /output unless your task explicitly says to. Use /tmp for scratch.
- If you build the Rust project with cargo, set CARGO_TARGET_DIR to a unique /tmp dir so parallel
  agents don't contend on the same target lock. rustc is 1.75.0; g++ 12 is at /opt/compiler/gcc-12/bin/g++.
- Zero external crates are allowed: std only. Rust edition 2021.
- Report facts you actually observed by running commands. Never claim a test passed without output.


Deliverable under review: /output (Rust port of /workspace/dataset/test2.cpp; reference binary /workspace/dataset/test2_executable).
Original C++ semantics:

indicators::ProgressBar — memory layout recovered from the ctor at 0x402790:
  @0x00 int progress          default 0
  @0x04 int total             default 100
  @0x08 int width             default 50   (movl $0x32)
  @0x0c bool show_percentage  default true
  @0x0d bool show_bar         default true   (gates opening glyph + body + closing glyph)
  @0x10 std::string fill      "="
  @0x18 std::string lead      ">"
  @0x20 std::string remainder " "
  @0x28 std::string start     "["
  @0x30 std::string end       "]"

std::string ProgressBar::str() const  — recovered from 0x402b20:
  std::ostringstream os;
  if (show_bar) {                       // cmpb 0xd(%rbx)
    os << start;
    int completed = (int)((float)progress / (float)total * (float)width);
        // cvtsi2ss progress; cvtsi2ss total; divss; cvtsi2ss width; mulss; cvttss2si
    for (int i = 0; i < width; ++i) {   // loop skipped entirely when width <= 0
      if (completed > i)       os << fill;
      else if (completed == i) os << lead;
      else                     os << remainder;
    }
    os << end;
  }
  if (show_percentage) {                // cmpb 0xc(%rbx)
    if (show_bar) os << " ";
    os << std::fixed << std::setprecision(1)
       << ((double)((float)progress / (float)total) * 100.0) << "%";
       // divss -> cvtss2sd -> mulsd 100.0 -> _M_insert<double>; precision=1, fixed flag 0x4
  }
  return os.str();

indicators::Percentage(float value): str() == fixed/setprecision(1) of ((double)value * 100.0), then "%".
  (In main the value is constant-folded to the double 75.0 at 0x403070.)

main() takes argc/argv and never reads them: set_progress(75); set_total(100); set_width(30);
cout << bar.str() << endl; Percentage pct(0.75f); cout << pct.str() << endl; return 0;
Program output is therefore invariant across all command lines:
  "[======================>       ] 75.0%\n75.0%\n"


Reported finding (from the "requirements-audit" pass):
  title: 13 MB pre-built binary artifact committed in the package root next to test2.rs
  file: /output/test2
  severity: minor
  detail: /output/test2 is a 13,298,464-byte unoptimized rustc output sitting in the Cargo package root. It currently produces byte-identical output, so it is not wrong today, but it is a build artifact shipped as part of the deliverable: it makes the deliverable ~13 MB, and it goes stale silently the moment test2.rs changes. Concretely, after applying the fix for the argv panic above, this stale binary would still panic on non-UTF-8 argv (verified: it exits 101 today), and a grader who runs `cargo build --release` and then `./test2` from /output would execute this stale root artifact rather than target/release/test2. Requirement 5 is still met -- /output/test2.rs is intact Rust source, not overwritten by the compiled artifact -- so this is hygiene, not a functional failure.
  evidence: $ ls -la --time-style=full-iso /output/
-rwxr-xr-x 1 root root 13298464 2026-08-10 12:36:29 test2
-rw-r--r-- 1 root root      808 2026-08-10 12:36:13 test2.rs

$ cmp <(/output/test2) <(/workspace/dataset/test2_executable) && echo CMP-OK-prebuilt
CMP-OK-prebuilt

$ /output/test2 $'\xff' >/dev/null; echo exit=$?
exit=101
thread 'main' panicked at library/std/src/env.rs:807:51:
called `Result::unwrap()` on an `Err` value: "\xFF"
  suggested fix: Delete /output/test2 and add a .gitignore with `/target` and `/test2` so the artifact is not re-committed. Graders can regenerate it with either documented command (`rustc test2.rs` or `cargo build --release`), both of which I confirmed work from a clean copy.

Judge it through the correctness lens — is the described behaviour actually wrong relative to the C++ binary/spec? Reproduce it by running commands.

Reproduce or refute by running commands. Do NOT modify /output. Then answer: is this real and worth
fixing? If real, state the minimal correct fix precisely (exact file, exact code change).

input.minimal_fix:
No fix required for this finding — nothing under /output should change on its account, and the suggested fix should be rejected. Deleting /output/test2 removes a working binary that currently matches the C++ reference byte-for-byte, and adding a `.gitignore` rule for `/test2` is both inert (no VCS exists; git is not installed) and self-contradictory with the deliverable's own documented `rustc test2.rs` build path, which writes the binary back to exactly /output/test2.

The only genuine correctness defect in this neighbourhood is the one the finding names in passing as separate — the non-UTF-8 argv panic, which lives in the source, not in the artifact. /output/test2.rs line 16:

    let _args: Vec<String> = std::env::args().collect();

C++ `main(int argc, char* argv[])` never reads argv, so the C++ binary exits 0 with the correct output for any command line, whereas `std::env::args()` panics on non-UTF-8 arguments (verified: exit 101). If that separate finding is actioned, the minimal change is to stop decoding argv at all — either drop line 16 entirely (nothing reads `_args`) or, to keep the parallel with argc/argv explicit, use the lossless non-panicking form:

    let _args: Vec<std::ffi::OsString> = std::env::args_os().collect();

If and only if that source edit is made, /output/test2 must be REBUILT (`cargo build --release && cp target/release/test2 ./test2`, or `rustc -O test2.rs -o test2`) so the shipped artifact stays in sync — rebuilt, not deleted. Fixing the source while leaving the old binary in place is the one scenario that would turn today's non-issue into a real staleness divergence.

